# <NAME> — AGENTS.md

Even Realities G2 smart-glasses app. Vite + TypeScript + `@evenrealities/even_hub_sdk`, served live by claude-hub — installable as a PWA, no `.ehpk` packaging in the loop.

## Workflow rule: commit + push every turn

Every turn that changes code, config, assets, or docs ends with a commit —
and a push if this project has a remote. Don't wait to be asked. One commit
per logical change; run the build/tests first and fix what fails before
committing. Restart the project's systemd unit when the running app needs the
change to take effect. Skip only when the turn produced no working-tree changes.

**Commit explicit paths, never `-A`.** Several Claude sessions can share this
checkout (one per Develop tab), so `git add -A` sweeps up whatever a peer
session has half-written. Name what you wrote:
`git commit -m "…" -- src/foo.ts src/foo.test.ts`.

## Workflow rule: git worktrees

Parallel work goes in a worktree, not in this checkout — two agents editing
one tree is the "peer swept my files" problem above, at feature scale. Claude
Code's `isolation: "worktree"` drops a checkout at `~/projects/<NAME>_<task>/`;
give it a `.project-meta.json` naming `worktreeOf: "<NAME>"` and its `branch`
and it becomes its own claude-hub card with its own port, terminal and Browse
pane, so it runs its own dev server instead of competing for this one's.

**Never `rm -rf` a worktree** — this repo's `.git/worktrees/` keeps the
registry entry and then refuses to reuse the path. Delete it from its card
(which routes through `git worktree remove`), or
`git -C ~/projects/<NAME> worktree remove --force <dir>`. A worktree also
checks out this project's `README.md` byte-for-byte, so its card title and
description have to come from its sentinel, not the README.

## Workflow rule: the spec is the memory (SDD)

`SPEC.md` at the root is this project's durable memory — goals (`§G`),
constraints (`§C`), interfaces (`§I`), invariants (`§V`), tasks (`§T`) and
bugs (`§B`), written compressed enough to reload on every request. It exists
because your context window resets and the code does not: anything decided but
not written down gets re-derived next session, differently. Read it before you
change anything; update it in the same turn as the code, never "later". The
loop is: read the spec → work against it → prove each `§V` you touched with a
named test → **backprop** — every bug becomes a `§B` row and its class becomes
a `§V` invariant, so the project stops re-making mistakes it has already made.

The part that needs discipline is not writing the spec, it is retiring what a
new requirement invalidated. `§V`/`§I` describe the present and get edited;
`§T`/`§B` are logs and only get appended. Numbers are permanent addresses —
never reused, even after retirement. Before appending an invariant, grep `§V`
for its subject: a rule that changed gets **revised in place at its existing
number**, tagged `(revised)` and carrying `⊥ <the old rule>` so nobody walks
back into it — a rule whose concern is gone gets **deleted**, its retirement
logged in the `§T` row that did the work.

**Full protocol: `~/projects/claude-hub/SDD.md`** — section reference, the
encoding and its symbol table, backprop, and the maintenance rules for keeping
the spec true as the project grows. Browse it at
`/view/claude-hub/SDD.md`.

## How this app reaches the glasses

There is no build-and-install step. claude-hub already serves this project at
`/<NAME>/`, and the Even App loads that URL straight into its WebView:

```bash
npm run qr -- "https://<gpu-host>/<NAME>/"   # prints a QR in this terminal
```

Scan it with the Even Realities app. The page runs on the phone, the SDK
bridge draws on the glasses, and Vite's HMR means an edit here lands on your
face a second later. `<gpu-host>` is this machine's tailnet name — the phone
has to be on the tailnet, and the URL must be the **https** one, because a
service worker (and therefore installability) needs a secure context.

Two consumers, one bundle:

| Where it runs | What it gets |
|---|---|
| Even App WebView | companion page **and** the glasses bridge |
| Any phone/desktop browser | companion page alone, installable to the home screen |

**A bridge is not proof of glasses.** The SDK installs a bridge object on
`window` in *any* browser and force-readies it after 100 ms, so
`waitForEvenAppBridge()` usually resolves even on a desktop with no Even App in
sight. The host's absence only shows up in the first call across the bridge:
`createStartUpPageContainer` answers with a non-zero code and the SDK logs
"Flutter handler not available". `startGlassesPage()` treats that as the
ordinary standalone case and returns `null` — companion-only is a normal state,
not an error to render.

**And never top-level-`await waitForEvenAppBridge()`.** The other direction
happens too: with no bridge object at all the promise never settles, and the
installed PWA sits on a blank screen forever. `src/glasses.ts` wraps it in
`connectBridge()`, a timeout race that returns `null` instead of hanging. Every
new entry path goes through it.

Packaging into an `.ehpk` (`evenhub pack`) is only for store distribution. If
you ever do it, build with a **relative** base (`vite build --base=./`) — a
packed app is loaded from the bundle root, not from `/<NAME>/`, and the
proxy-shaped base would 404 every asset.

## PWA

- `public/manifest.webmanifest` — `start_url` and `scope` are `/<NAME>/`, the
  same string as `base` in `vite.config.ts` and `proxyPrefix` in
  `.project-meta.json`. Change one, change all three or the install breaks.
- `public/sw.js` — deliberately cache-nothing, same shape as claude-hub's own
  service worker. It exists so the phone offers "Add to Home Screen". This page
  is a live view onto a dev server; a cache here would serve a stale bundle to
  the glasses and read as a code bug.
- `public/icon.svg` — placeholder mark, SVG because claude-hub's `copyTemplate`
  reads template files as UTF-8 and would corrupt a PNG. Your project is not a
  template: drop real 192/512 PNGs into `public/` and list them in the manifest
  when you want a proper iOS home-screen icon.

## Glasses UI, the short version

- Canvas is **576 x 288**, 4-bit greyscale (16 shades of green). No colour, no
  camera, no speaker.
- `src/glasses.ts` owns everything SDK-shaped: one full-screen text container,
  event routing, teardown. `src/main.ts` owns the phone page.
- Events: taps/double-taps/lifecycle arrive on `event.sysEvent`, scroll on
  `event.textEvent` — never mix them. `CLICK_EVENT` is `0` and protobuf omits
  zero-valued fields, so a single tap arrives with `eventType` undefined; the
  default must be resolved inside the envelope check, not after it.
- Double-tap must always reach `shutDownPageContainer` — it is the user's only
  way out of the app.
- Deeper API/design references live in the `everything-evenhub` plugin skills
  (`/everything-evenhub:sdk-reference`, `:glasses-ui`, `:design-guidelines`,
  `:handle-input`, `:device-features`).

## app.json

The Even Hub manifest. Not needed to run via QR, but it is where permissions
are declared (`network`, `g2-microphone`, `phone-microphone`, `location`,
`album`, `camera`), and `evenhub pack` validates it hard:

- `package_id` — reverse-domain, **lowercase letters and digits only**, no
  hyphens, each segment starting with a letter. Scaffolded as
  `com.example.<NAMESLUG>`; change `example` to something you own.
- `name` — **20 characters max**.
- `edition` — must stay `"202601"`.

## claude-hub conventions

- **Dev server** runs via `vite@<NAME>.service` (systemd, `Restart=always`). Don't `npm run dev` manually — the unit owns the port.
- **Reverse-proxied** at `/<NAME>/` by claude-hub. `vite.config.ts` sets `base: '/<NAME>/'` and `server.port: <PORT>` to match `.project-meta.json`.
- **Entry point** is `src/main.ts`. No framework — plain TS and the SDK.
- **Card metadata** comes from `README.md`: H1 = title, first paragraph = description, frontmatter `tags: [...]` = badges and the landing page's filter chips. Reuse a tag already on the hub (the chip row above the cards lists them) rather than coining a new one; add a tag only if none fits. Tags are categories, not status — no `WIP`.

## What to do first

1. Ask the user what to build.
2. Update `README.md` (H1, first paragraph, tags) and `app.json` (`name`, `package_id`).
3. Edit `src/glasses.ts` (what the G2 shows) and `src/main.ts` (what the phone shows).
4. New deps: `npm install <pkg>` here; the unit reloads on file change.

## Restarting the dev server

```bash
sudo systemctl restart vite@<NAME>.service
journalctl -u vite@<NAME>.service -f
```

## Testing without hardware

`npm run simulate` runs the desktop simulator against this project's dev
server. It needs a display on the machine it runs on — over a browser terminal
you will usually be better off scanning the QR onto the real glasses.
